    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d352">

                <pb facs="0673" n="577" xml:id="pg577"/>

                <head>No. 350.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bead
                        to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-04-03"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 3, 1874</date>.
                        (Received April 30.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 15.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I have
                    been requested by many highly respectable and influential citizens to pray the
                    Government not to increase the duty upon dried currants. * * * A number of Greek
                    and American merchants have lately learned that a proposition is before the
                    House of Representatives to double this importation tax.</p>
                <p>Commending the subject anew to the favorable notice of the Department, and
                    referring to previous elaborate communications upon this subject,</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN MEREDITH READ, <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
